{
  "index": 5,
  "response": " In Python 3, the bitwise left shift operator `<<` shifts the bits of a number to the left by a specified number of places. If the left operand (in this case, `x`) is an integer and the right operand (`3`) is also an integer, the operation will shift `x`'s binary representation 3 places to the left, effectively multiplying it by 2 raised to the power of 3 (which equals 8).\n\nGiven that `x = 1`, in binary form this is `0b0001`. After shifting it left 3 places, the binary representation becomes `0b00010000`. In decimal form, `0b00010000` equals 8.\n\nSo, if you write the code as follows:\n\n```python\nx = 1\nresult = x << 3\nprint(result)\n```\n\nThe output will be `8`.",
